{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "expand-fueltype-diesel-runspec",
    "description": "PLAN.md §7.5 step 7d, the SOURCE TYPE dimension at its smallest discriminating width: the `expand-fueltype-diesel` run computed from the DEFAULT DATABASE through `moves-onroad.esm`. It selects two source types, 21 (passenger car) and 32 (light commercial truck), and is otherwise `process-tirewear`'s scope with `expand-day`'s pollutant-processes.\n\nWHAT IT DISCRIMINATES, which §23 asks for before a dimension is called covered.\n\n* THE SOURCE TYPE AS AN AXIS. Every other RunSpec in this tree selects one, so until now a model that summed `runspecsourcetype` into a scalar passed the whole suite -- and one did: `run_sourceTypeID` was exactly that sum. The drive cycle is the deep half. Vehicle-specific power is computed from the source type's own row of `sourceusetypephysics`, so VSP, the operating modes it bins into, the speed-bin brackets and the operating-mode distribution the rates are weighted by are all per source type, and a run at width two computes two of each.\n\n* A COHORT COUNT THAT IS A SUM AND NOT A PRODUCT. 248 rows, not 250: source type 21 carries 125 surviving (modelYearID, fuelTypeID) cohorts and source type 32 carries 123. Both select the same four fuels, so the difference is in `samplevehiclepopulation` -- two candidates have no sample vehicle and are not emitted. Nothing in the model declares 248. `runspecsourcefueltype` is the cohort grid's second factor and carries (sourceTypeID, fuelTypeID) PAIRS, `coh_rank` densifies whatever survives, and `cohortSurvivorCount` recomputes the total, so the ragged count arrives as one number that the shape check holds the emitted relation to.\n\nTHE TEN SELECTIONS, ONE BINDING AND THE ORACLE are everything this document carries. Against `runspecs/expand-day.esm` it differs in eleven `url_template`s and nothing else -- same binding, same structure -- which is the measurement PLAN.md §7.1 wanted: a second source type costs a per-fixture document nothing, because the cost was paid once in the domain model.\n\nWHY THIS DOCUMENT IS IN `runspecs/pending/` AND NOT `runspecs/`, as of 2026-09-18, and for a DIFFERENT reason since the source bin landed. It is complete and it validates; the model does not yet reproduce it, for one reason that is neither the source-type axis nor the source bin. The vehicle physics `sourceusetypephysics` holds is keyed by (sourceTypeID, regClassID, model-year range), and this document's drive cycle is per SOURCE TYPE: `phys_rollingTermA` and the rest are a `sum_product` over the rows a source type selects. Source type 21 selects ONE, so the distinction was invisible for every run before this one. Source type 32 selects THREE -- regulatory class 30, and regulatory class 41 split at model year 2010 because its fixed mass factor changes from 2.05979 to 5.0 -- and the model's own guard says so: `run_physicsRowsSelected` is 1 for the first of this run's source types and 3 for the second, where the variable's description says the day it is not 1 `dss_vsp` is the sum of two vehicles' physics rather than one's.\n\nWHAT IS ALREADY PROVEN BY RUNNING IT, which is why it is checked in rather than deleted. The emitted relation is the right SHAPE -- 248 rows, 125 under source type 21 and 123 under 32, from a declaration the chain recomputes. Every one of source type 21's 125 rows reproduces `MOVESOutput` at worst cell 7.106e-06, the same worst cell `expand-day` reports. The SOURCE-HOUR activity is exact for BOTH source types, to every digit of the execution database's own `sho` table. And the SOURCE-BIN DISTRIBUTION is exact for both: 330 selected bins, 125 under source type 21 and 205 under 32, every `bin_usedFraction` equal to the run's own `sourceBinDistributionFuelUsage_1_26161_2020.sourceBinActivityFraction` and every per-(source type, model year) total exactly 1. Three of the four factors of an emission are right; the fourth is the operating-mode distribution, and it is wrong by a constant per fuel -- 1.7316 on E85, 2.1633 on electricity, 1.48 to 3.06 where two regulatory classes blend.\n\nWHAT IT WAITS ON: the drive cycle becoming per PHYSICS ROW rather than per source type. `sourceusetypephysics` has 178 rows over 13 source types, 1 for a motorcycle or a passenger car and 15 to 21 for a heavy truck; this run selects 4 of them and `expand-sourcetype` selects 40. Everything from `phys_isSelected` down to `pp_W` carries a `runspecsourcetype_rows` axis today and would carry that one instead, which makes it the same promotion 7d did for the source type, one key finer and over the model's largest relational step. PLAN.md §7.3.6 has the measurement.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "runspec",
      "onroad",
      "default-database",
      "expand-day"
    ]
  },
